Consumes COGAL-02, #7 and #11; coordinates with #62/#19.

## Objective

Define how the pre-language cognitive algebra becomes **tokenizable semantic identities and deterministic initial neural geometry** for the first Bilqis learner.

The objective is not to assign arbitrary integer token IDs early. It is to freeze semantic identities, relational geometry and compilation rules so #11 can assign a token ABI and #62 can deterministically initialize the learner.

## Required semantic geometry

The compiler should preserve at least:

- semantic family and subtype;
- operator arity;
- argument/role structure;
- identity/equality/exclusion relations;
- implication/dependency direction;
- commutativity/symmetry where applicable;
- inverse/negation relations;
- truth-functional behavior;
- quantifier/binder behavior;
- proof-rule applicability;
- prerequisite ancestry;
- sibling/contrast relations;
- known composition identities;
- epistemic-state distinctions;
- graph neighborhood over the frozen T0-T4 kernel.

## Ternary boundary

The intended learner may use ternary-forward weights.

The initializer MAY exploit signed geometry for relations that legitimately admit directional/contrastive structure, and zero-valued structure for absence/sparsity, but:

- semantic truth state is not weight state;
- UNKNOWN is not numerically defined as weight zero;
- FALSE is not numerically defined as weight -1;
- TRUE is not numerically defined as weight +1;
- conflict/ambiguity must remain representable independently of weight quantization.

#62 remains owner of `semantic_aligned / iid_random / semantic_permuted` profiles.
#19 remains owner of ternary forward/training precision semantics.

## Required outputs

- cognitive-algebra feature-map schema for #62;
- deterministic semantic-object → feature-vector transform;
- declared geometry for logical inverses, siblings, composition and proof relations;
- mapping from semantic IDs to #11 token identities without hard-coding tokenizer implementation into #9;
- reserved-token policy for future algebra expansion;
- pre-step geometry checks suitable for #42;
- distortion checks if ternary/low-precision paths quantize any initialized parameter class.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every T0-T4 pasigraph can compile to the #62 feature-map input.
- [ ] Equivalent semantics do not receive conflicting geometry solely because they have different surface forms.
- [ ] Negation/inverse/contrast relationships are machine-readable.
- [ ] UNKNOWN/CONFLICT/ambiguity remain geometrically and semantically distinguishable.
- [ ] Semantic permutation preserves vector multiset while breaking identity alignment.
- [ ] Token-ID permutation remains distinguishable from semantic permutation.
- [ ] Ternary precision cannot silently reinterpret semantic state.
- [ ] #42 can inspect the exact pre-optimizer geometry.

## Dependencies

COGAL-02, #7, #11.  
Coordinates with #62, #19, #42.

## Workability

`EXECUTABLE_LEAF`: one bounded compilation/geometry contract.

## Publication posture

Public research/architecture contract. No protected training-optimization method is disclosed.

## Parent

Parent: #9.

## Objective

Assemble the **complete semantic substrate Bilqis is permitted to possess before its first developmental training run** and freeze the corresponding token/geometry manifest.

This is the integration point between:
- COGAL-02 formal logic;
- COGAL-07 necessary mathematics;
- COGAL-06 Rosetta epistemic/process semantics;
- any narrowly approved pre-Ithkuil semantic factors required to close Rosetta expressivity gaps;
- #7 semantic ABI;
- #11 token ABI;
- #62 deterministic semantic initialization.

## Invariant

The full pre-language identity/geometry map is frozen **before** training begins, even though curriculum exposure is staged.

Staged teaching does not require the project to invent semantic identities mid-run.

## Required output

Produce one immutable pre-language substrate manifest containing:

- semantic identity registry;
- operator/type signatures;
- prerequisite graph;
- canonical AST projections;
- relation graph;
- composition identities;
- Rosetta primitive/composite mappings;
- token identities assigned by #11;
- #62 semantic feature vectors / initialization refs;
- reserved/future token policy;
- source/provenance hashes;
- exact curriculum-visible subset per stage.

## Ternary boundary

The manifest may be used to initialize a ternary-forward learner, but semantic state and ternary weight state remain distinct.

The geometry may intentionally encode similarity, opposition, support, exclusion, dependency, arity, role or graph structure. It MUST NOT define truth simply as `-1/0/+1`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Every pre-language curriculum object resolves to a frozen semantic identity.
- [ ] All admitted identities compile through #7/#11/#62.
- [ ] Stage visibility can be varied without changing the underlying identity map.
- [ ] Reserved/future identities cannot collide with the frozen tranche.
- [ ] Rosetta semantics survive deterministic round-trip projection.
- [ ] The manifest supports the first ternary learner without introducing task-capable pretrained weights.
- [ ] #42 can inspect the exact initial geometry before optimizer step one.
- [ ] Later New Ithkuil expansion has an explicit versioned extension path rather than silently redefining this substrate.

## Dependencies

#7, #11, #62, COGAL-02, COGAL-06, COGAL-07.

## Downstream

#82 developmental plan lock, #18/#19 learner path, #42 geometry audit, #52 first local learning run.

## Workability

`EXECUTABLE_LEAF`: one bounded frozen pre-language substrate manifest.
